# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: pinned tool versions, clang-format, a search for switched-off
checks (in the source, its preprocessor directives, after macro expansion and in the syntax tree),
a build with clang-tidy and warnings as errors, a check of the build's own compile commands that
every project file gets the gates, and the tests with AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
and LeakSanitizer. Every line of a Unity test file must run. The git hook and CI run it
too. It works from any folder and any shell: it starts itself under `mise exec`, and the tools see
none of the caller's environment variables.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/check`, `cmake/gates.cmake`, `CMakePresets.json`, `.clang-tidy`, `.clang-format`,
  `mise.toml`, `mise.lock`, `third_party/`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.claude/`, and existing
  tests.

```sh
mise exec -- cmake --build --preset dev                  # build after scripts/check ran once
mise exec -- ctest --preset dev -R {{c_name}}_test       # one test executable by name
scripts/format                                           # format every C file
# Search longer with a random seed. LeakSanitizer is off by default on macOS.
ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1 build/dev/{{c_name}}_property -max_total_time=60 -rss_limit_mb=0
build/dev/{{c_name}}_property <crash-file>               # replay a failing input
```

Sandboxed agents: mise writes to `~/.local/share/mise`, `~/.local/state/mise` and its cache
(`~/Library/Caches/mise` on macOS, `~/.cache/mise` on Linux). The first `mise install` on a machine
and `gh` need the network. Without these, `scripts/check` fails for reasons outside the code.

## C

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing C.

- C23, no compiler extensions. Use `nullptr`, `bool`, `[[nodiscard]]` and `static_assert`.
- Every public name starts with `{{c_name}}_` and is declared in a header under `include/`.
  Everything else in a `.c` file is `static`.
- No variable-length arrays. Every implicit narrowing or sign change is an error: check the range,
  then convert explicitly.
- Check the result of every library call that can fail (`fclose`, `fwrite`, `snprintf`, ...).
  Use the result of every project function, static helpers too, or drop it on purpose with
  `(void)`.
- The library never calls `exit` or `_exit`: it returns an error to its caller. `abort()` is for
  broken invariants only.
- Braces on every branch (clang-format adds them). No `#pragma` except `#pragma once`, no `#line`
  and no asm labels.
- Use only headers from the C library, the OS or a declared dependency. The check rejects headers
  that come from the compiler's own folder.

## Layout

- Logic goes in the library: `include/{{c_name}}.h` and `src/`. `app/main.c` only calls it.
- `test/` holds Unity example tests and libFuzzer property tests. Tests are `static` functions that
  `main` runs with `RUN_TEST`. Every line of a Unity test file must run, so a test that `main`
  never runs, an early `return` or a branch a test never takes fails the check. A test file has
  no `#if` or `#ifdef`, and does not define a function of the library it tests (tests link the
  library whole, so a stub fails the link). Do not `#define` or `#undef` Unity's names, `setUp`,
  `tearDown`, `abort`, `exit` or a `{{c_name}}_` function in `test/`.
- Register tests in `CMakeLists.txt` with `add_unity_test(name file libs...)` or
  `add_property_test(name file libs...)`, after the static libraries they test. The check rejects a
  test made any other way, a test property such as `WILL_FAIL` or `ENVIRONMENT`, a change to the
  variables the helpers use (`CMAKE_COMMAND`, `GATES_LLVM_COV`), and a `CTestCustom.cmake` in the
  build folder.
- Every new `.c` file goes into a target in `CMakeLists.txt`, after
  `include(cmake/gates.cmake)`, that the default build makes. Include only `.h` and `.inc` files
  that git tracks. New dependencies need the owner's approval.
- A Unity test passes only when it exits 0 and ends with Unity's summary of passed tests:
  `return UNITY_END();` ends `main`.
- A property test runs a fixed seed and run count in `scripts/check`, and passes only when
  libFuzzer ran every input. It defines no `main` and no `LLVMFuzzerInitialize`. CI searches longer with a random seed every week
  (`.github/workflows/weekly.yml`). A failing input becomes an example test.
- `clangd` reads `build/dev/compile_commands.json`. Run `scripts/check` once to create it.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
